Using JSTL (JavaServer Pages Standard Tag Library) and EL (Expression Language) in JSP pages allows for a 
more dynamic and efficient way to work with data. These technologies make it easier to process and display 
data from a Servlet, JavaBeans, or request parameters in a readable and concise manner without writing Java code inside the JSP page.

Below, I will guide you through an example where we use JSTL tags and EL to display user data on a JSP page.
1. Setup of Dependencies (JSTL and EL)
If you are using Maven, you can add the following dependency for JSTL:

<dependency>
    <groupId>javax.servlet.jsp.jstl</groupId>
    <artifactId>jstl</artifactId>
    <version>1.2</version>
</dependency>

Otherwise, you can manually include the following JARs in your WEB-INF/lib directory:

jstl-1.2.jar
standard-1.1.2.jar


